{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01620 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Pheniramine, a histamine antagonist, is implicated in the treatment of Nasopharyngitis, which in turn is associated with the Common Cold. The evidence suggests that Pheniramine may exert a therapeutic effect through a chain of indications that ultimately lead to the Common Cold. This pathway involves Pheniramine being linked to Nasopharyngitis, which is indicated for Guaiacol and Bromodiphenhydramine, both of which are also linked to the Common Cold. The presence of multiple independent pathways connecting Pheniramine to the Common Cold through different intermediate entities supports a potential mechanistic link, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The integration of these pathways implies that Pheniramine may influence the course of the Common Cold via a complex network of physiological interactions, though further research is needed to confirm the precise role of each step in this chain.",
